## Why This Is Asked
Checks understanding of bitwise ops, table-driven CRC and streaming data handling.

## Key Concepts
- CRC32 fundamentals
- Lookup-table optimization
- Streaming updates and finalization
- Endianness considerations

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>
static uint32_t crc32_table[256];

void init_crc32_table(void){
  const uint32_t poly = 0xEDB88320U;
  for (int i=0;i<256;i++){
    uint32_t c = i;
    for (int j=0;j<8;j++) c = (c & 1) ? (c >> 1) ^ poly : (c >> 1);
    crc32_table[i] = c;
  }
}

uint32_t crc32_update(uint32_t crc, const uint8_t* data, size_t len){
  crc ^= 0xFFFFFFFFU;
  for (size_t i=0;i<len;i++){
    crc = crc32_table[(crc ^ data[i]) & 0xFF] ^ (crc >> 8);
  }
  return crc ^ 0xFFFFFFFFU;
}

```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for incremental calls with chunked input sizes?
- How does endianness affect the final CRC value on different architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-thread buffering, cache-line alignment, and atomic merges to minimize contention in high-frequency workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Thread-local buffers to avoid contention
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Atomic merges with memory_order_relaxed for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdalign.h>
#define NBINS 256

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned long long bins[NBINS];
} thread_hist_t;

unsigned long long global_bins[NBINS];

void add_sample(thread_hist_t* t, unsigned int v) {
  t->bins[v]++;
}

void merge_all_to_global(thread_hist_t* t) {
  for (int i = 0; i < NBINS; ++i)
    __atomic_fetch_add(&global_bins[i], t->bins[i], __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // optionally clear t->bins here
}
```

